## 5. Content Outline

---

**H1:** What to Ask When You Tour an Apartment

---

**H2: The Questions Worth Asking, Organized by Category**

*Writing instruction:* Open with a 40-60 word direct answer to the keyword query. State that the questions fall into five categories. This paragraph is the featured snippet target and the AI citation anchor. Do not write more than 60 words in this opening block before moving to the first category. Every sentence in this block must be independently useful.

*Example opening structure (do not copy verbatim, use as reference):* "When touring an apartment, the most useful questions fall into five categories: cost and fees, lease terms and policies, maintenance, building and unit condition, and the surrounding area. Covering all five before you leave the tour prevents the most common post-move surprises and gives you a complete picture of what you are actually committing to."

---

**H2: Cost and Total Monthly Fees**

*Writing instruction:* Cover the full cost picture beyond listed rent. Include: base rent confirmation, utilities (which are included, which are not, average monthly estimates), parking costs, pet fees (distinguish between pet deposit and pet rent, as this is a content gap competitors blur), amenity fees, and security deposit terms. Include the specific question to ask the leasing agent for each item. Do not editorialize. Give the question, give one sentence on why it matters.

**H3: Ask About Utilities Separately from Rent**

*Writing instruction:* Introduce and define the distinction between master-metered buildings (all units share one meter, costs are split or estimated) and individually metered units (you pay exactly what you use). This is a content gap no competitor in Data Source 1 explains clearly. State: "In master-metered buildings, your utility estimate reflects an average, not your actual usage. Ask whether the unit is individually metered." One short paragraph, 3-4 sentences.

---

**H2: Lease Terms and Policies**

*Writing instruction:* Cover lease length options (month-to-month vs. fixed term), early termination penalty (ask for the exact dollar amount or formula, not just whether a penalty exists), lease renewal process and notice requirements, subletting policy, and roommate addition process. Frame each as the exact question to ask. Mention that early termination fees are often equal to 1-2 months of rent, which is a specific benchmark competitors rarely provide.

---

**H2: Maintenance and Building Operations**

*Writing instruction:* Cover how maintenance requests are submitted (portal, phone, email), whether staff is on-site or contracted out, average response time for non-emergency requests, emergency maintenance availability (24/7 or not), and whether planned renovations are scheduled for the unit or building. Include the question: "Has this unit had any water damage, mold, or pest issues in the past two years?" Explain that in some states landlords are required to disclose this. One sentence on disclosure requirements.

---

**H2: Building Condition: What to Look for Without Asking**

*[CONTENT GAP — see Section 6d]*

*Writing instruction:* This section addresses what to observe during the tour without relying on the leasing agent's answers. This is a content gap: most competitors list questions to ask but do not give renters a physical inspection checklist. Include: check under sinks for moisture or staining, test water pressure in the shower and kitchen, flush every toilet, check window operation (stuck windows reflect deferred maintenance), look at outlets and light switches (outdated or discolored covers reflect older electrical), check cell signal on your carrier inside the unit. Frame each as a one-sentence observational instruction. No questions to ask the agent here. This is what you observe yourself.

---

**H2: Policies That Affect Daily Life**

*Writing instruction:* Cover guest policy (maximum consecutive nights; many properties limit to 7-14 days), quiet hours, smoking policy (unit, balcony, and common areas), package delivery handling (locker system, front desk, or unmanaged), and permission to make modifications (paint, drill, mount hardware). For each, give the specific question to ask. Keep each item to 2-3 sentences.

---

**H2: The Area Around the Apartment**

*Writing instruction:* Per Fair Housing rules, describe the surrounding area strictly by lifestyle infrastructure. Do not reference crime statistics, safety language, or neighborhood demographics. Ask about: proximity to grocery stores, transit stops (and which lines), coffee shops and restaurants within walking distance, fitness options, and parking for guests. Include the question: "Are there any planned construction projects on this block or adjacent blocks in the next 12 months?" Explain that nearby construction is one of the least-disclosed issues in apartment tours and can mean 18-36 months of noise.

---

**H2: How to Prepare Before the Tour**

*Writing instruction:* Short, practical section (150-200 words). Cover: bring a notes app or printed checklist rather than relying on memory, ask to see the actual unit you would rent rather than a model unit (this is a standard tactic leasing offices use), schedule the tour during the time of day you would most often be home (morning, evening) so you hear actual ambient noise levels, and bring your phone to test cell signal inside the unit. This section answers the PAA question "How do I prepare for an apartment tour?"

---

**H2: Frequently Asked Questions**

*Writing instruction:* Include 6 Q&A pairs. Each answer must be 40-60 words (featured snippet length). Use FAQPage schema on these. Questions sourced from PAA data (Data Source 2) and AI engine gaps (Data Source 3). See Section 7c, FAQ Rule for the specific questions.

